/**
 * The short plain-text briefing about one building that is sent to the vision
 * model alongside the annotated floor plans on every route request.
 *
 * ### Why this exists
 *
 * A photographed floor plan shows what is *on* a floor but almost nothing about
 * how floors relate. Nothing on the image says that the west stairwell stops at
 * level 3, that `E3` is the only sensible shaft for the east wing, or that the
 * second digit of a room number encodes the wing rather than the floor. Those
 * facts are what cross-floor routes get wrong, and a dozen lines of prose here
 * fix far more mistakes than a better prompt does.
 *
 * It is deliberately **prose, not structure**. The model reads it as context, so
 * authoring it is a demo-tuning activity: walk a route that comes out wrong, work
 * out which unstated fact would have prevented it, add that line. A normalized
 * schema of shafts and wings would be more satisfying and much slower to iterate
 * on, and the deterministic {@link NavRouter} already covers the case where
 * structure is genuinely required.
 *
 * One row per {@link Building}; id convention `facts-<buildingId>`.
 */
entity type BuildingFacts {

  /**
   * The building these facts describe. One row per building.
   */
  building: !Building

  /**
   * The briefing itself — a dozen or so lines of plain text or light markdown,
   * injected verbatim into the cached prefix of the model request.
   *
   * Should cover, for this building: which shafts reach which floors and which
   * are step-free; what the room-numbering scheme encodes; how the wings are laid
   * out; which entrances exist; and explicitly **which floors are not mapped**, so
   * the model answers "I don't have that floor" instead of inventing waypoints
   * for it.
   *
   * Keep it short. This text sits in front of every request, and vagueness here
   * costs accuracy on exactly the cross-floor routes that are worth demoing.
   */
  facts: !string

  /**
   * The curated, verified example prompts offered to the user as tappable chips.
   *
   * These are the routes that have been walked in person and confirmed correct, so
   * they are both the demo script and the regression suite. They are also the set
   * eligible for the offline fallback — see {@link RouteQuery#isPinned}.
   */
  demoRoutes: [string]
}
